            %p#InTermsOfStringDiagrams
              In terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
              the above data entering the definition looks like
            %p
              %img{:alt => "String diagram for a left adjoint (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-L.png"}>/
                      
              %img{:alt => "String diagram of a right adjoint (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-R.png"}/
                      
              %img{:alt => "String diagram of an adjunction unit (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-unit.png"}/
                      
              %img{:alt => "String diagram of an adjunction co-unit (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-co-unit.png"}/
            %p
              (where 1-cells read from right to left and 2-cells from bottom to top), and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag+identities"} zig-zag identities
              appear as moves “pulling zigzags straight” (hence the name):
            %p
              %img{:alt => "String diagram of first zigzag identity (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-up-string.png"}>/
                      
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-down-string.png"}/
            %p
              Often, arrows on strings are used to distinguish
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %p
              %img{:alt => "Minimal string diagram of first zigzag identity (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-up-string-minimal.png"}>/
                      
              %img{:alt => "Minimal string diagram of second zigzag identity (for 'Adjunction')", :src => "/nlab/files/adjunction-down-string-minimal.png"}/
            %p
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_monads Relation to monads
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "monad#RelationBetweenAdjunctionsAndMonads"} monad – Relation between adjunctions and monads
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#AdjointFunctors Adjoint functors
          %p
          #AdjunctionsInCatAreAdjointFunctors.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              An adjunction in the 2-category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              is equivalently a pair of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
          See also the proof
          %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#AdjointnessInTermsOfHomIsomorphismEquivalentToAdjunctionInCat"} here
          at
          = succeed "." do
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Suppose given functors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \,\colon\, C \to D
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: D \to C
              and the structure of a pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
              in the form of a natural isomorphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
              (
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"}> here
              )
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Psi_{c, d}
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\hom_D(L(c), d) \cong \hom_C(c, R(d))
            %p
              Now the idea is that, in the spirit of the (proof of the)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
              to be determined by what it does to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction+unit"} adjunction unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon 1_C \to R L
              by the formula
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                          %mo ,
                          %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_c = \Psi_{c, L(c)}(1_{L(c)})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
